WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.418 --> 00:00:03.503
On March 30, 2017,

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:06.006
Kendrick Lamar dropped
his video for "Humble."

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.425
It was a visual marvel.

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:11.177
In a sea of one eye-catching
shot after another,

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:15.223
it was this 10-second
stretch from 1:57 to 2:07

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:17.100
that people couldn't stop talking about.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.060
♪ Ay, Obama just paged me, ay ♪

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:20.645
♪ I don't fabricate it, ay ♪

00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:22.605
♪ Most of y'all be fakin', ay ♪

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.983
Today, you can see these
seemingly sporadic,

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:27.235
but precise movements
in a bunch of projects,

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:29.279
like Doja Cat's video for "Boss B----."

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:31.031
♪ I wear the hat, and I wear the pants ♪

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:32.866
♪ I am advanced so I get advance ♪

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:34.868
Kygo and Miguel's "Remind Me to Forget."

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:38.872
♪ Love, keep reminding me, oh ♪

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.666
And Jay Rock and Kendrick
Lamar's "Wow Freestyle."

00:00:41.666 --> 00:00:43.209
♪ Lil' man mad, I don't wanna go bad on ♪

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:44.919
♪ Big heat stamp fold
out, when you lack on ♪

00:00:44.919 --> 00:00:46.296
It suddenly seems like hip-hop

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:48.048
can't get enough of this type of shot

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:50.842
captured by this robotic camera rig.

00:00:50.842 --> 00:00:52.677
Sick. [laughs]

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:54.054
But there was a time when this technology

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:56.222
was mostly used for food commercials.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.142
To understand how the cinebot went

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:00.518
from food-commercial staple

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:02.312
to avant-garde music video tool,

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:04.522
we've gotta rewind a few decades.

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:07.317
Music videos have been
around since the 1950s,

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:10.445
but at the stroke of
midnight on August 1, 1981,

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:12.614
a new era in music began.

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:15.909
MTV, the first-ever 24-hour
music video channel,

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:17.494
aired for the first time.

00:01:17.494 --> 00:01:20.080
It played The Buggles'
"Video Killed the Radio Star"

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.289
as its first video.

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:24.709
♪ Now I understand the
problems you can see ♪

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:26.336
Labels began throwing money into creating

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:28.963
visual components to
help boost album sales,

00:01:28.963 --> 00:01:31.633
and the music channel was
the perfect vessel for that.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.219
And 15 years after MTV's arrival,

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:36.346
there was a new player
in the video-making arena

00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:38.681
capitalizing on the music-video boom.

00:01:38.681 --> 00:01:41.559
This is Jason Rau, the managing
director of Camera Control,

00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:44.104
who's been there since the
company's very beginning.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:45.855
Jason Rau: Back in the day, all the big,

00:01:45.855 --> 00:01:48.650
you know, Britney Spears,
Justin Timberlake,

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.028
all those big music videos, we worked on.

00:01:52.028 --> 00:01:53.905
Narrator: And they used
motion-controlled rigs,

00:01:53.905 --> 00:01:56.032
or cinebots, to make it happen,

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:58.326
though not exactly in the same way.

00:01:58.326 --> 00:01:59.994
One of the earliest video projects to use

00:01:59.994 --> 00:02:02.747
a motion-controlled camera
system on a big scale

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:04.749
was a long, long time ago.

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:08.670
More specifically, in the
original 1977 "Star Wars" movie.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:09.921
[pistol fires]

00:02:09.921 --> 00:02:12.715
In early sci-fi films,
planet and spaceship models

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:14.050
would move around the camera.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.427
But digital effects pioneer John Dykstra

00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:18.555
created the Dykstraflex, which allowed

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:20.890
for the camera to slowly
move around the model.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.350
This better simulated movement

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.103
while still keeping the
shot perfectly stable.

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:27.689
Compared to more modern
motion-controlled rigs,

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:29.357
like the Bolt or the Cobra,

00:02:29.357 --> 00:02:31.359
the Dykstraflex was very large,

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:34.404
moved very slowly, and its
angle movement were limited.

00:02:34.404 --> 00:02:36.114
Despite its limits in hindsight,

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:39.993
this new rig system completely
revolutionized video capture.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:41.744
It was one of the first
times camera movements

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:45.206
could be programmed into an,
albeit, very large computer,

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:48.293
so they could be precisely
replicated take after take.

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:51.087
This allowed editors to
add in multiple composites,

00:02:51.087 --> 00:02:53.006
making arguably the most iconic

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:55.341
intergalactic battle scene of its time.

00:02:56.259 --> 00:02:58.511
From there, this technology
entered more parts

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:00.430
of the video industry, from commercials

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.057
to smaller-scale projects,
like music videos.

00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:05.476
Enter Camera Control,
which used the technology

00:03:05.476 --> 00:03:07.687
to create some of the
most iconic music videos

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.148
of the '90s and early 2000s.

00:03:10.148 --> 00:03:11.316
For the early projects,

00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:13.109
Jason says the motion-controlled movements

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:16.446
were more static, used
mainly for super-precise pans

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:18.615
and to create slow-motion effects,

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:20.950
like in this Britney
Spears video from 2000.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:21.993
Simon Wackley: So, we used the slower,

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:23.453
larger rigs for motion control,

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:25.914
but the faster rigs
really had their heyday

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:27.624
in the last four or five years.

00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:28.875
They've been used more and more.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.252
Well, I think a lot of the previous stuff

00:03:31.252 --> 00:03:34.797
was heavy post-effects, heavy compositing.

00:03:34.797 --> 00:03:36.174
And now I think it's more about

00:03:36.174 --> 00:03:37.884
that dynamic camera move.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.719
You go from ground level to high up

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.804
in, you know, a second or less.

00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:44.057
Narrator: But this advancement
in music-video technology

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:45.350
didn't come immediately.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.852
At the dawn of Y2K, the
music-video industry

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:49.896
faced its own mini apocalypse.

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:51.147
The whole music industry

00:03:51.147 --> 00:03:53.149
was pretty much flipped upside down.

00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:56.069
Physical media sales were
slowly becoming nonexistent.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:58.112
Between 2000 and 2010,

00:03:58.112 --> 00:04:01.824
CD sales dropped $9.8 billion in revenue.

00:04:01.824 --> 00:04:04.619
That's because more consumers
started getting music online,

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:07.288
oftentimes illegally
through sites like LimeWire.

00:04:07.288 --> 00:04:09.582
A lawsuit brought against LimeWire alleged

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:11.668
that LimeWire alone cost record labels

00:04:11.668 --> 00:04:14.254
about $500 million a month in sales

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:17.590
and that users stole about
3 billion songs per month.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.135
Jason: There were now new
digital platforms for music.

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:23.680
So the industry as a
whole was very confused

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.474
and just trying to figure
itself out and reinvent itself.

00:04:26.474 --> 00:04:28.685
Narrator: At the time, Jason
says filming music videos

00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:30.561
back then was a lot more expensive.

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:32.689
Cinematographers were shooting on film,

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:35.608
and productions needed bigger
crews to see projects through.

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:38.361
With this loss of income
from low record sales,

00:04:38.361 --> 00:04:40.863
labels just weren't
investing in videos too much.

00:04:40.863 --> 00:04:43.449
Jason: The slump in the
music-video industry

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:46.494
really didn't affect us
because we are involved

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:48.955
in many other facets of the industry.

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.373
We do a lot of feature film work,

00:04:50.373 --> 00:04:52.125
a lot of commercial work.

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:53.710
Narrator: During the early 2000s,

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.129
Camera Control shifted
its shoots away from music

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:59.048
to feature films and
commercials using cinebots.

00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:01.301
And food commercials were, and still are,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.761
huge users of cinebot technology.

00:05:03.761 --> 00:05:06.723
Jason: We do a lot of what's
called tabletop shooting,

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:10.560
and one of the main reasons
why this technology is used

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.646
in that part of the industry
is the precise control

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:15.398
that you have over the camera,

00:05:15.398 --> 00:05:18.776
especially when you're shooting
at very high frame rates.

00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:20.528
Narrator: High frame rates
are used to prevent blur

00:05:20.528 --> 00:05:22.572
on fast-moving subjects, especially when

00:05:22.572 --> 00:05:24.449
you want to create slow-motion effects.

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:26.659
But you need a lot of
stability to do it right.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:29.412
Jason: Imagine you're shooting
at 1,000 frames a second

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:32.457
and you're trying to track
something falling through the air

00:05:32.457 --> 00:05:35.710
and splashing into a glass of water.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:38.796
Trying to do that by hand would
be super, super difficult.

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:40.173
Narrator: Meanwhile, music videos were

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:41.591
on the verge of a comeback.

00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:43.843
Labels finally found ways to comfortably

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:46.304
capitalize on streaming in the 2010s

00:05:46.304 --> 00:05:48.306
with the help of the advertising industry

00:05:48.306 --> 00:05:50.141
and subscription services.

00:05:50.141 --> 00:05:53.603
As of 2018, streaming
accounted for more than 75%

00:05:53.603 --> 00:05:55.355
of all music consumption,

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:56.856
and ad revenue from streaming sites

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.816
helped to refill industry pockets.

00:05:58.816 --> 00:06:01.361
At the same time, though,
YouTube, a new platform,

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:03.321
was moving past fun cat videos

00:06:03.321 --> 00:06:04.864
and babies biting their siblings.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.574
Harry: Ouch!

00:06:06.574 --> 00:06:08.117
Narrator: Record companies
were finding new ways

00:06:08.117 --> 00:06:10.703
to capitalize on the
seemingly endless exposure

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:12.914
that online video had to offer,

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:14.749
and with new video-publishing technology

00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:17.085
came new video-capturing technology.

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:19.879
Camera Control would soon be
back in the music-video game.

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:21.881
Jason: Music videos have
come back in a big way,

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:25.426
for financial reasons and
for technical reasons.

00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:28.763
Now you've got digital camera systems

00:06:28.763 --> 00:06:30.556
that have become super small,

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:33.893
less expensive to purchase,
less expensive to rent.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.520
Narrator: And with less
expensive technology

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.397
and fewer financial restrictions,

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:40.066
video directors like
"Humble" director Dave Meyers

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:42.610
reached out to Jason to
push visual boundaries.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.279
That call would revolutionize
not only Jason's business,

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:47.073
but the hip-hop music video.

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:48.991
Meyers' crew attached smaller cameras

00:06:48.991 --> 00:06:50.868
to cinebots, like the Bolt or Cobra,

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:52.578
and programmed them to move electronically

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:55.540
at super-precise angles around a subject.

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.626
The cinebot's fast-moving
tracking capability

00:06:58.626 --> 00:07:01.587
is the core of what
makes "Humble" so iconic.

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:03.840
After the video was released,
the response was huge.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.550
Not only to the video and the song,

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:06.717
but to the camera work.

00:07:06.717 --> 00:07:08.761
Jason: After everyone
sort of saw that video,

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:10.680
we were getting calls and a lot of people

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.348
referencing that video for the type

00:07:12.348 --> 00:07:14.267
of movements that they wanted to do.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:15.810
Narrator: And since that watershed moment

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:17.645
when "Humble" dropped, Camera Control

00:07:17.645 --> 00:07:20.481
has worked with artists like
ASAP Rocky and Post Malone,

00:07:20.481 --> 00:07:22.275
and even some outside
of the hip-hop arena,

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:24.610
like K-pop group ONF.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.779
When properly executed,
filming with cinebots

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.365
offers surreal,
larger-than-life perspectives.

00:07:29.365 --> 00:07:32.118
Cinematographers can control
speeds in the foreground

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:33.995
and the background of any given shot.

00:07:33.995 --> 00:07:35.746
And because the camera's
programmed to make

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:37.457
and repeat precise movements,

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:38.916
editors have multiple versions

00:07:38.916 --> 00:07:40.877
to play with after filming ends.

00:07:40.877 --> 00:07:42.336
It's this versatility that makes

00:07:42.336 --> 00:07:44.547
the motion-controlled rigs so desirable

00:07:44.547 --> 00:07:47.800
and why the technology will
be around for the long run.

